(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[19] = {"n":19,"set":"B","level":"Intermediate","band":"B1 40% · B2 60%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Rainwater harvesting","p":["In many parts of the world, water shortages are becoming one of the most serious challenges of the twenty-first century. Growing populations, expanding cities and changing rainfall patterns are putting pressure on rivers, lakes and underground aquifers. One simple and ancient solution is receiving renewed attention: rainwater harvesting, the practice of collecting and storing rainwater for later use.","People have harvested rainwater for thousands of years. In the ancient Middle East, communities carved large cisterns into rock to store water from winter rains. The Nabataeans of Petra and Hegra built sophisticated systems of channels and tanks that allowed them to live in desert regions. In parts of India, traditional stepwells, deep structures with stairs leading down to the water, collected monsoon rain and supplied villages throughout the dry season.","A modern rainwater harvesting system is usually quite simple. Rain falls on a roof or another hard surface, called the catchment area. Gutters and pipes carry the water to a storage tank, which may be above or below the ground. Before the water enters the tank, a filter removes leaves, dust and other debris. Many systems also include a 'first flush' device, which diverts the first few litres of rain away from the tank, because this water carries most of the dirt from the roof.","The amount of water that can be collected is surprisingly large. Each millimetre of rain that falls on one square metre of roof produces about one litre of water. This means that a house with a roof of one hundred square metres in an area with five hundred millimetres of annual rainfall could, in theory, collect around fifty thousand litres of water a year. Even in dry regions, where rain is rare but often heavy, a well-designed system can capture a useful amount.","Harvested rainwater has many uses. It is most commonly used for watering gardens, flushing toilets and washing cars, which can reduce household demand for treated drinking water significantly. With proper treatment, such as filtering and disinfection, it can even be made safe to drink. On a larger scale, rainwater harvesting can help reduce flooding in cities, because water is stored instead of running off roads into drains.","There are, however, some limitations. Systems require an initial investment, regular cleaning and maintenance, and enough space for storage tanks. In very dry areas, long periods without rain may leave tanks empty for months. Nevertheless, as part of a wider strategy that includes water saving and recycling, rainwater harvesting offers a practical way for households, schools and businesses to make better use of a precious resource."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of Petra","how to build a house","causes of flooding in cities","collecting and using rainwater"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains rainwater harvesting, past and present.","h":"حصاد مياه الأمطار."},{"n":2,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Nabataeans and Indian stepwells?","o":["to compare two religions","to describe famous tourist sites","to explain how monsoons form","to show that rainwater harvesting is an ancient practice"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 2 gives historical examples.","h":"ممارسة قديمة."},{"n":3,"s":"What is the 'catchment area'?","o":["the storage tank","the filter","the surface on which rain falls","the pipe to the garden"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Paragraph 3 defines it.","h":"سطح التجميع."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does a 'first flush' device divert the first few litres of rain?","o":["That water carries most of the dirt from the roof.","It is too cold to store.","It contains too much salt.","The tank is not ready yet."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"تحمل معظم الأوساخ."},{"n":5,"s":"How much water does one millimetre of rain on one square metre produce?","o":["about ten litres","about one litre","about one hundred litres","about half a litre"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"لتر واحد تقريبًا."},{"n":6,"s":"A roof of one hundred square metres receiving two hundred millimetres of rain a year could collect about","o":["fifty thousand litres","two hundred litres","two thousand litres","twenty thousand litres"],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"100 m² × 200 mm × 1 litre = 20,000 litres.","h":"عشرون ألف لتر."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a use of harvested rainwater?","o":["watering gardens","producing electricity","flushing toilets","washing cars"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Electricity is not mentioned.","h":"توليد الكهرباء غير مذكور."},{"n":8,"s":"How can rainwater harvesting reduce flooding in cities?","o":["Water is stored instead of running off into drains.","It makes roofs stronger.","It increases rainfall.","It cleans the streets."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"تخزين الماء بدل جريانه."},{"n":9,"s":"The word 'Nevertheless' in the last paragraph introduces","o":["another limitation","a positive conclusion despite the limitations","an example of a failed system","a historical fact"],"k":1,"ty":"organization","e":"It contrasts the limitations with the overall value.","h":"خلاصة إيجابية رغم القيود."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's overall view of rainwater harvesting?","o":["It is a practical part of a wider water strategy.","It can solve all water problems alone.","It is too expensive to be useful.","It should only be used in ancient cities."],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The last sentence states this view.","h":"جزء عملي من استراتيجية أوسع."}]},{"t":"How the pyramids were built","p":["The pyramids of Giza, built more than four and a half thousand years ago, are among the most famous structures in the world. The largest, the Great Pyramid of King Khufu, was originally about one hundred and forty-six metres tall and remained the tallest human-made structure on Earth for nearly four thousand years. For centuries, people have wondered how the ancient Egyptians, without modern machines, were able to build such enormous monuments.","The Great Pyramid contains around 2.3 million blocks of stone, most of them limestone, with an average weight of about two and a half tonnes. Some granite blocks inside the pyramid weigh more than fifty tonnes. Most of the limestone was cut from quarries close to the building site, while the finer white limestone used for the outer surface came from Tura, across the Nile, and the granite came from Aswan, about eight hundred kilometres to the south.","Recent discoveries have helped explain how this material was transported. In 2013, archaeologists found papyrus documents at Wadi al-Jarf on the Red Sea coast. These documents, the oldest inscribed papyri ever found, include the logbook of an official named Merer, who supervised a team that transported limestone blocks by boat along the Nile and through canals to Giza. They show that the Egyptians used the river and a network of artificial waterways to move heavy stones over long distances.","Once the blocks reached the site, workers probably moved them on wooden sledges. A wall painting from a later tomb shows a worker pouring water on the sand in front of a sledge carrying a large statue. Physicists have shown that wetting sand in this way can reduce the force needed to pull a heavy object by about half. To raise the blocks, the builders probably used ramps made of mud brick and rubble, although historians still debate whether these ramps were straight, zigzagged or spiralled around the pyramid.","Who built the pyramids? A popular belief is that they were built by slaves, but archaeological evidence suggests otherwise. Excavations near Giza have uncovered the remains of workers' villages, bakeries and cemeteries. The workers' tombs, close to the king's pyramid, suggest that they were respected. Many were skilled craftsmen employed all year round, supported by a larger number of farmers who worked on the project for part of the year, perhaps during the annual flood when they could not farm their fields.","Although many questions remain, the pyramids show the impressive organizational skills of the Egyptian state: planning, managing thousands of workers, supplying them with food and coordinating transport over hundreds of kilometres. In many ways, these achievements are as remarkable as the pyramids themselves."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What question does the passage mainly address?","o":["Why were the pyramids painted?","Who destroyed the pyramids?","Where are the newest pyramids?","How were the pyramids built?"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"Paragraph 1 introduces the question.","h":"كيف بُنيت الأهرامات؟"},{"n":12,"s":"Where did the granite blocks come from?","o":["Giza","Tura","Wadi al-Jarf","Aswan"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 states this.","h":"أسوان."},{"n":13,"s":"Why are the Wadi al-Jarf papyri important?","o":["They contain a map of the pyramid.","They describe the king's life.","They show how stone was transported by water.","They prove that slaves built the pyramids."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Merer's logbook describes transporting limestone by boat.","h":"تبين نقل الحجارة بالماء."},{"n":14,"s":"The word 'supervised' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["was in charge of","paid","worked under","replaced"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Merer was in charge of a team.","h":"أشرف."},{"n":15,"s":"What does the wall painting suggest about moving heavy objects?","o":["Wetting the sand made pulling easier.","Statues were carried by animals.","Sledges were made of stone.","Workers moved objects only at night."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Wetting sand reduces the force needed.","h":"ترطيب الرمل يسهّل السحب."},{"n":16,"s":"What do historians still debate, according to paragraph 4?","o":["whether ramps were used at all","where the limestone came from","how tall the pyramid was","the shape of the ramps"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Straight, zigzagged or spiral ramps are debated.","h":"شكل المنحدرات."},{"n":17,"s":"What evidence suggests that the workers were not slaves?","o":["They were paid in gold.","They wrote complaints.","Their tombs were located close to the king's pyramid.","They came from other countries."],"k":2,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Their tombs near the pyramid suggest respect.","h":"قبورهم قرب الهرم."},{"n":18,"s":"Why might farmers have worked on the pyramids during the flood?","o":["The flood made transport impossible.","They could not farm their fields at that time.","The king forced them to leave their homes.","They were paid more during the flood."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 5 suggests this.","h":"لم يستطيعوا الزراعة."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the Egyptian state's organization?","o":["planning","managing thousands of workers","supplying food","collecting taxes from other countries"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Foreign taxes are not mentioned.","h":"الضرائب الخارجية غير مذكورة."},{"n":20,"s":"What point does the writer make in the last paragraph?","o":["The organization behind the pyramids was itself remarkable.","All questions about the pyramids are answered.","The pyramids were built quickly.","Modern machines could not build pyramids."],"k":0,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"The achievements are 'as remarkable as the pyramids themselves'.","h":"التنظيم مذهل."}]},{"t":"Responsible tourism","p":["Tourism is one of the world's largest industries. Before the pandemic, international tourist arrivals reached about one and a half billion a year, and tourism provided around one in ten jobs worldwide. It can bring enormous benefits, creating employment, supporting local businesses and encouraging understanding between cultures. However, when it is poorly managed, tourism can also damage environments, communities and the very places that attract visitors.","One of the most visible problems is overtourism, when too many visitors come to a place at the same time. In cities such as Venice and Barcelona, residents have complained that crowds, rising rents and short-term holiday rentals have changed their neighbourhoods and pushed local people out. Popular natural sites can also suffer: footpaths are eroded, wildlife is disturbed, and beaches and coral reefs are damaged by pollution and careless behaviour.","Another concern is that much of the money spent by tourists may not benefit local communities. When visitors stay in international hotel chains, eat in foreign-owned restaurants and book through overseas companies, a large share of their spending leaves the country. Economists call this 'leakage'. In some destinations, local workers are employed only in low-paid jobs, while the profits go elsewhere.","Responsible tourism aims to address these problems. It encourages travellers, businesses and governments to make choices that minimize harm and maximize benefits for local people and the environment. For tourists, this can mean staying in locally owned accommodation, eating at local restaurants, buying handmade products directly from artisans and hiring local guides. It also means respecting local customs, dressing appropriately and asking permission before photographing people.","Destinations can also manage tourism more carefully. Some have introduced limits on visitor numbers, timed tickets or entry fees that are used to protect sites. Others encourage visitors to travel outside peak seasons or to explore lesser-known areas. In Saudi Arabia, which has opened to international tourism in recent years, major projects such as those on the Red Sea coast have announced goals to protect coral reefs, limit the number of visitors and rely on renewable energy.","Critics point out that responsible tourism can sometimes be used as a marketing label without real change. Long-distance flights, for example, still produce large carbon emissions. Nevertheless, supporters argue that thoughtful choices by individual travellers and careful planning by destinations can make tourism a positive force. As one travel writer has put it, the goal should be to leave places better than we found them."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Tourism should be stopped completely.","Tourism has no disadvantages.","Tourism should be managed to reduce harm and increase benefits.","Only rich countries benefit from tourism."],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage presents problems and responsible solutions.","h":"إدارة السياحة بمسؤولية."},{"n":22,"s":"What is 'overtourism'?","o":["too many visitors coming to a place at the same time","tourism outside peak seasons","travel by local people","a type of hotel"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Paragraph 2 defines it.","h":"السياحة المفرطة."},{"n":23,"s":"Why have residents of Venice and Barcelona complained?","o":["There are not enough tourists.","Crowds and rising rents have changed their neighbourhoods.","Hotels have closed.","Their beaches have disappeared."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"الزحام وارتفاع الإيجارات."},{"n":24,"s":"What do economists mean by 'leakage'?","o":["water lost from hotels","tourist spending that leaves the country","information shared online","visitors leaving early"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains it.","h":"خروج الإنفاق السياحي."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following is NOT suggested as responsible behaviour for tourists?","o":["eating at local restaurants","hiring local guides","staying in international hotel chains","asking before photographing people"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"International chains contribute to leakage.","h":"الفنادق العالمية ليست منها."},{"n":26,"s":"How can entry fees help protect tourist sites?","o":["They keep all visitors away.","They make sites more popular.","They pay for flights.","The money can be used to protect them."],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Fees that are used to protect sites.","h":"تمويل الحماية."},{"n":27,"s":"Why does the writer mention projects on the Red Sea coast?","o":["to show that tourism damages coral","to advertise hotels","as an example of a destination planning responsible tourism","to compare Saudi Arabia with Venice"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"They aim to protect reefs and limit visitors.","h":"مثال على التخطيط المسؤول."},{"n":28,"s":"What criticism of responsible tourism is mentioned?","o":["It is too cheap.","It can be used as a marketing label without real change.","It ignores local customs.","It only applies to cities."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"The last paragraph mentions this criticism.","h":"مجرد شعار تسويقي."},{"n":29,"s":"Which sentence best paraphrases 'the goal should be to leave places better than we found them'?","o":["Tourists should never return to the same place.","Tourists should visit only new places.","Places should be closed after tourists leave.","Tourists should try to improve the places they visit."],"k":3,"ty":"paraphrase","e":"Same meaning in different words.","h":"إعادة صياغة."},{"n":30,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards tourism?","o":["entirely negative","entirely positive","balanced, recognizing both benefits and problems","uninterested"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The passage presents benefits, problems and solutions.","h":"متوازن."}]},{"t":"Octopus intelligence","p":["When people think of intelligent animals, they usually imagine chimpanzees, dolphins or dogs. Few would think of an octopus. Yet these eight-armed sea creatures, which are closely related to snails and clams, have shown abilities that have amazed scientists and challenged ideas about how intelligence develops.","Octopuses have a remarkably large nervous system for an invertebrate, an animal without a backbone. The common octopus has around five hundred million neurons, similar to the number found in a dog. Surprisingly, about two thirds of these neurons are located not in the brain but in the arms. Each arm can taste, touch and even make some decisions on its own, while the central brain coordinates the overall behaviour. This unusual design means that an octopus thinks in a very different way from humans.","Octopuses are famous for their problem-solving skills. In laboratory experiments, they have learned to open jars to reach food inside and to find their way through simple mazes. Some have escaped from their tanks by squeezing through tiny gaps, because their bodies have no bones, and returned after eating fish from neighbouring tanks. Researchers have also observed octopuses in the wild carrying coconut shells and using them as portable shelters, an example of tool use that was once thought to be limited to mammals and birds.","One of the octopus's most impressive abilities is camouflage. Its skin contains thousands of special cells called chromatophores, which allow it to change colour and pattern in a fraction of a second. Some species can also change the texture of their skin to imitate rocks, coral or seaweed. The mimic octopus goes even further: it can copy the shape and movements of other, more dangerous animals, such as poisonous fish and sea snakes, to scare away predators.","There is also evidence that octopuses can recognize individual humans. In one aquarium, an octopus repeatedly sprayed water at one particular staff member, while behaving calmly with others. Such observations suggest that octopuses can learn and remember complex information about their environment.","These discoveries have raised ethical questions. Because octopuses appear to be intelligent and may feel pain, some countries have begun to include them in animal welfare laws. At the same time, plans to farm octopuses for food have been criticized by scientists who argue that these curious, solitary animals are poorly suited to life in crowded tanks. Studying the octopus not only teaches us about a fascinating creature but also makes us think about how we treat other animals."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"Why might people not usually think of octopuses as intelligent?","o":["They are related to snails and clams.","They live in fresh water.","They have small bodies.","They are rarely seen."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"The passage contrasts them with familiar intelligent animals.","h":"لارتباطها بالحلزون."},{"n":32,"s":"What is unusual about the octopus nervous system?","o":["It has no brain.","It has fewer neurons than a snail.","Most of its neurons are in its arms.","All neurons are in its head."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"About two thirds are in the arms.","h":"معظم الخلايا في الأذرع."},{"n":33,"s":"The word 'invertebrate' in paragraph 2 refers to","o":["an animal that lives in water","an animal without a backbone","an animal with eight arms","an animal that can change colour"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The passage defines it.","h":"لافقاري."},{"n":34,"s":"How have some octopuses escaped from their tanks?","o":["by squeezing through tiny gaps","by breaking the glass","by jumping very high","by opening the door"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Their bodies have no bones.","h":"بالتسلل من فتحات صغيرة."},{"n":35,"s":"Why is the use of coconut shells significant?","o":["It shows that octopuses eat coconuts.","It proves octopuses live on land.","It shows that octopuses build houses.","It shows tool use in an animal that is not a mammal or bird."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Tool use was once thought limited to mammals and birds.","h":"استخدام الأدوات."},{"n":36,"s":"What are chromatophores?","o":["arms used for tasting","a type of predator","skin cells that allow colour change","bones in the octopus's body"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines them.","h":"خلايا تغيير اللون."},{"n":37,"s":"How does the mimic octopus protect itself?","o":["by hiding in coconut shells","by copying dangerous animals","by spraying water","by swimming very fast"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"It copies poisonous fish and sea snakes.","h":"تقليد الحيوانات الخطرة."},{"n":38,"s":"What does the aquarium story suggest?","o":["Octopuses dislike all humans.","Octopuses can recognize individual people.","Octopuses cannot see well.","Octopuses enjoy playing with water."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"It sprayed one staff member but not others.","h":"التعرف على الأفراد."},{"n":39,"s":"Why have some scientists criticized octopus farming?","o":["Octopuses are poorly suited to life in crowded tanks.","Octopuses are too expensive to feed.","Octopus meat is unhealthy.","There are already too many octopuses."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"The last paragraph explains this.","h":"لا تناسبها الأحواض المزدحمة."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the purpose of the last paragraph?","o":["to describe octopus camouflage","to explain how octopuses are cooked","to discuss ethical questions raised by octopus intelligence","to compare octopuses and dolphins"],"k":2,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It discusses welfare laws and farming.","h":"القضايا الأخلاقية."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"My brother ______ as a nurse since he finished his degree.","o":["has been working","works","is working","worked"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"has been working — Duration since a past point: present perfect continuous.","h":"المضارع التام المستمر."},{"n":42,"s":"A new law ______ to protect endangered animals last year.","o":["introduced","has introduced","was introduced","was introducing"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was introduced — Past passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول."},{"n":43,"s":"You ______ bring your passport. Your ID card is enough.","o":["don't need to","mustn't","can't","shouldn't to"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"don't need to — No necessity.","h":"لا حاجة."},{"n":44,"s":"The company ______ I work for is based in Dammam.","o":["who","that","where","whose"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"that — that for things (defining).","h":"that."},{"n":45,"s":"If I ______ his phone number, I would call him now.","o":["know","knew","had known","will know"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"knew — Second conditional.","h":"الشرط الثاني."},{"n":46,"s":"We're looking forward to ______ the new museum.","o":["visit","visited","be visit","visiting"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"visiting — look forward to + -ing.","h":"بعد to هنا -ing."},{"n":47,"s":"I need to have my passport ______ before the trip.","o":["renew","renewing","renewed","to renew"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"renewed — Causative.","h":"التركيب السببي."},{"n":48,"s":"The faster you drive, ______ dangerous it becomes.","o":["the most","the more","more","most"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the more — the + comparative, the + comparative.","h":"كلما … كلما."},{"n":49,"s":"He ______ have missed the bus. He's never late.","o":["can't","should","needn't","must"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"must — Logical deduction about the past.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":50,"s":"I wish I ______ more time to read for pleasure.","o":["have","had","would have had","will have"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had — Present wish.","h":"تمنٍّ."},{"n":51,"s":"By the end of the decade, our region ______ four new universities.","o":["will build","builds","is building","will have built"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have built — Future perfect.","h":"المستقبل التام."},{"n":52,"s":"The customer asked whether the shop ______ on Fridays.","o":["opens","will open","has opened","opened"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"opened — Reported question: tense moves back.","h":"الكلام المنقول."},{"n":53,"s":"______ she was very busy, she helped me with my project.","o":["Even though","Despite","In spite of","Because of"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Even though — Contrast clause.","h":"Even though."},{"n":54,"s":"It was ______ an exciting match that nobody left their seat.","o":["so","too","such","very"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"such — such + an + adjective + noun.","h":"such an."},{"n":55,"s":"The majority of the students ______ in favour of the new timetable.","o":["was","is","were","has"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — The majority of + plural noun: plural verb.","h":"the majority of + جمع."},{"n":56,"s":"She ______ to working long hours, so she doesn't mind.","o":["is used","used","uses","was use"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is used — be used to + -ing.","h":"be used to."},{"n":57,"s":"The library, ______ was built in the 1980s, needs to be renovated.","o":["that","who","which","what"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — Non-defining clause for things.","h":"which."},{"n":58,"s":"Never ______ such a beautiful view.","o":["I have seen","I saw","seen I have","have I seen"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have I seen — Never at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Never."},{"n":59,"s":"He offered ______ me to the airport.","o":["driving","to drive","drive","drove"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to drive — offer + to + verb.","h":"offer + to."},{"n":60,"s":"Unless you ______ now, you'll miss the train.","o":["leave","will leave","left","don't leave"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"leave — unless + present for future condition.","h":"unless."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) police (B) is (C) investigating the (D) accident.","o":["police","is","investigating","accident"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"is — police takes a plural verb: are.","h":"police جمع."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) has (B) lived in Abha (C) for ten years and (D) love it.","o":["has","lived","for","love"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"love — she is third person singular: loves.","h":"loves."},{"n":63,"s":"(A) Although the delay, the (B) plane (C) landed (D) safely.","o":["Although","plane","landed","safely"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Although — A noun needs Despite: Despite the delay.","h":"Despite مع الاسم."},{"n":64,"s":"If I (A) had (B) known, I (C) would come (D) earlier.","o":["had","known","would come","earlier"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"would come — Third conditional: would have come.","h":"would have come."},{"n":65,"s":"He (A) denied (B) to take the money (C) from (D) the drawer.","o":["denied","to take","from","the"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to take — deny + -ing: taking.","h":"deny + -ing."},{"n":66,"s":"The (A) amount of (B) students (C) applying has (D) increased.","o":["amount","students","applying","increased"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"amount — students is countable: The number of.","h":"number مع المعدود."},{"n":67,"s":"This is (A) the (B) most (C) interesting book I have (D) ever read it.","o":["the","most","interesting","ever read it"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"ever read it — it repeats the object: I have ever read.","h":"لا نكرر الضمير."},{"n":68,"s":"I (A) wish I (B) can (C) speak French (D) fluently.","o":["wish","can","speak","fluently"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"can — wish + could for present ability.","h":"wish + could."},{"n":69,"s":"No sooner (A) had we (B) arrived (C) when it started (D) to rain.","o":["had","arrived","when","to rain"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"when — No sooner … than (not when).","h":"No sooner … than."},{"n":70,"s":"(A) There is a lot of (B) people (C) waiting (D) outside.","o":["There is","people","waiting","outside"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"There is — people is plural: There are.","h":"There are."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M19-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"Why isn't Lina on the bus?","o":["She missed the bus.","She forgot her permission form.","She went with her mother.","She is ill."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'Lina has a fever and won't be coming today.'","h":"مريضة."}],"t":"A missing student from a field trip list","sc":["Situation: Two teachers are checking a list before a school trip. (55 words)","Woman: I've counted the students on the bus. There are twenty-nine, but the list says thirty.","Woman: Who's missing?","Woman: Lina Al-Ghamdi. Her name is on the list, but I haven't seen her this morning.","Woman: Oh, her mother called the office earlier. Lina has a fever and won't be coming today. I forgot to cross her name off."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M19-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"Why can the man get a full refund?","o":["The course has been cancelled.","He is cancelling more than seven days before the course.","He has a medical certificate.","He paid in cash."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Today is eight days before the course.","h":"ألغى قبل سبعة أيام."}],"t":"Asking for a refund on a course","sc":["Situation: A man speaks to a training centre receptionist. (70 words)","Man: I registered for the photography course starting next week, but I've been transferred to another city for work. Can I get a refund?","Woman: Our policy is to give a full refund if you cancel at least seven days before the course starts. Today is eight days before, so that's fine.","Man: That's a relief. How long will it take?","Woman: The money will be returned to your card within ten working days."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M19-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"Why does the machine probably need descaling?","o":["Minerals from hard water have built up inside.","Someone dropped it.","It has no electricity.","It is very old."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'The water here is very hard, and minerals build up inside.'","h":"تراكم المعادن."}],"t":"A coffee machine out of order","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are in an office kitchen. (53 words)","Man: The coffee machine is broken again. It's showing a red light.","Man: It probably needs descaling. The water here is very hard, and minerals build up inside.","Man: Do you know how to do it?","Man: Yes, but it takes about forty minutes. Let's use the café downstairs for now, and I'll do it at lunchtime."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M19-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why did the man buy a used car?","o":["He could not get a loan.","New cars were sold out.","He could get a better model for the same price.","His father gave it to him."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"New cars lose value quickly; he got a better model.","h":"طراز أفضل بالسعر نفسه."}],"t":"A friend's new car","sc":["Situation: Two friends are standing next to a car. (52 words)","Man: Is this your new car? It looks great!","Man: Thanks. It's not new, actually. It's three years old, but it's in excellent condition.","Man: Why didn't you buy a brand-new one?","Man: New cars lose a lot of value in the first two years. This way, I got a better model for the same price."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M19-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What should the woman do before the meeting?","o":["call the manager","prepare a presentation","book another meeting","send her plan to the manager"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'Please send him your plan by Thursday'.","h":"إرسال الخطة."}],"t":"Booking a meeting with a manager","sc":["Situation: An employee speaks to a manager's assistant. (57 words)","Woman: Hello. I'd like to book a meeting with Mr. Saud to discuss my training plan.","Man: He's fully booked this week, I'm afraid. The earliest I can offer is next Monday at nine.","Woman: That's fine. How long will the meeting be?","Man: Thirty minutes. Please send him your plan by Thursday so he can read it before the meeting."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M19-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Why doesn't the bus stop at the museum this month?","o":["The museum is closed.","The road is closed for construction.","The bus has a new driver.","There are too many passengers."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"The road is closed for metro construction.","h":"إغلاق الطريق."}],"t":"A changed bus route","sc":["Situation: A passenger speaks to a bus driver. (53 words)","Woman: Excuse me, does this bus still stop at the National Museum?","Man: Not this month. The road near the museum is closed for metro construction. The nearest stop is now at the King Abdulaziz Library.","Woman: Is that far from the museum?","Man: About ten minutes on foot. There are signs to show you the way."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M19-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"How can the visitor find the X-ray department?","o":["by asking at reception","by taking the stairs to the first floor","by following a blue line on the floor","by reading a map at the entrance"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'follow the blue line on the floor'.","h":"اتباع الخط الأزرق."}],"t":"Asking for directions inside a hospital","sc":["Situation: A visitor asks a hospital volunteer for help. (50 words)","Man: Excuse me, where is the X-ray department?","Woman: Take the lift to the second floor, turn left and follow the blue line on the floor. It will take you straight there.","Man: Thank you. Is there a place to buy water on that floor?","Woman: There's a machine next to the waiting area."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M19-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What is the problem?","o":["The woman is sitting in the wrong class.","Her ticket has expired.","She has no ticket.","She is on the wrong train. Longer conversations"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Her ticket is for economy, but she is in business class.","h":"تجلس في الدرجة الخطأ."}],"t":"A ticket inspector on a train","sc":["Situation: A ticket inspector speaks to a passenger. (47 words)","Man: Tickets, please.","Woman: Here you are. I bought it on the app.","Man: Thank you. This ticket is for economy class, but you're sitting in business class.","Woman: Oh, I'm sorry. I didn't notice. Should I move?","Man: You can move to carriage four, or pay the difference and stay here."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M19-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why should episodes be about fifteen minutes long?","o":["The microphones only record fifteen minutes.","The school timetable requires it.","Students lose interest in longer episodes.","The scientist has little time."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'Students lose interest if it's much longer.'","h":"يفقدون الاهتمام."},{"n":80,"s":"Why do they want students to send questions?","o":["to save time recording","so that students feel involved","to test students' knowledge","to find a scientist to interview"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"'so they feel involved'.","h":"للمشاركة."}],"t":"Planning a science podcast","sc":["Situation: Two students are planning a podcast for their school. (141 words)","Woman: Our science podcast needs a clear format. How long should each episode be?","Woman: About fifteen minutes, I think. Students lose interest if it's much longer.","Woman: Agreed. And what topics should we start with?","Woman: Something connected to everyday life. Maybe 'Why does the sky look blue?' or 'How do vaccines work?'","Woman: Good. We could also interview a scientist in each episode. My aunt is a researcher at the university.","Woman: That would make it more interesting. But we need good sound quality, or people won't listen.","Woman: The school media room has proper microphones. We can record there after school.","Woman: And let's ask students to send questions for the next episode, so they feel involved.","Woman: Great idea. I'll design a short form for questions and share it on the school website. Let's record the first episode before the end of the month."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M19-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"Why do the friends decide not to compete on price?","o":["Their coffee is already cheap.","Customers don't care about price.","Prices are fixed by law.","The coffee chain is much bigger than them."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'They're much bigger than us.'","h":"السلسلة أكبر."},{"n":82,"s":"What is the purpose of hosting events?","o":["to make the café a community place","to sell more books","to train new staff","to reduce costs"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"'to make the café a community place'.","h":"جعل المقهى ملتقى للمجتمع."}],"t":"Discussing a marketing plan for a café","sc":["Situation: Two friends who own a small café are talking. (142 words)","Man: Business has been slow since the new coffee chain opened across the street.","Man: We can't compete on price. They're much bigger than us.","Man: Then we should focus on what makes us different. Our coffee beans come from Jazan, and we bake everything ourselves.","Man: True. We could tell that story on social media, with photos of the farms and our kitchen.","Man: And what about a loyalty card? The tenth coffee free.","Man: Good. We could also host small events, like book clubs or local artists' exhibitions, to make the café a community place.","Man: I like that. People come for the coffee but stay for the atmosphere.","Man: Let's start with the social media posts this week and plan the first event for next month.","Man: Agreed. I'll talk to the farmer in Jazan about sending us some photos and maybe a short video of the harvest."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M19-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Which of the following is included in the residence fee?","o":["bus tickets","books","a private car","meals, internet and cleaning"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph explains what is included.","h":"الوجبات والإنترنت والتنظيف."},{"n":84,"s":"Why does the student choose to live on campus?","o":["It is cheaper.","He doesn't like cooking.","It will be easier during his first year.","His friends live there. Talks and lectures"],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'it would be easier to live on campus while I get used to university life'.","h":"أسهل في السنة الأولى."}],"t":"Choosing a university housing option","sc":["Situation: A new student speaks to a housing advisor. (157 words)","Man: I'm starting university in September. What housing options are there?","Woman: You can live in the student residence on campus or rent a shared apartment off campus.","Man: What are the differences?","Woman: The residence is more expensive, but meals, internet and cleaning are included, and you're five minutes from your classes.","Man: And the apartments?","Woman: They're cheaper, but you'll need to cook, pay bills and travel to campus by bus, which takes about twenty-five minutes.","Man: In my first year, I think it would be easier to live on campus while I get used to university life.","Woman: That's what most first-year students choose. Many move to apartments in their second year, when they know the city better and have made friends to share with.","Man: That sounds sensible. How do I apply for a room in the residence?","Woman: Fill in the online form before the end of June. Rooms are given in the order applications are received, so don't wait too long."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M19-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"What happens when some plants are attacked by insects?","o":["They stop growing completely.","They release chemicals that warn nearby plants.","They lose all their leaves.","They move away from the insects."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"They release volatile organic compounds.","h":"تطلق مواد تحذيرية."},{"n":86,"s":"What do fungi receive from plants in the underground network?","o":["water","light","insects","sugars"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'The fungi receive sugars from the plants'.","h":"السكريات."},{"n":87,"s":"What does the lecturer say about the idea of trees deliberately helping each other?","o":["It may have been exaggerated.","It has been completely proven.","It is impossible.","It only applies to tomatoes."],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'has been exaggerated, and … more research is needed'.","h":"ربما مبالغ فيها."}],"t":"Lecture: how plants communicate","sc":["Situation: A botany lecturer speaks to students. (230 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Plants cannot move or speak, so it's easy to imagine that they live in isolation. However, research over the past few decades has shown that plants communicate in surprising ways. One of the best-known examples involves chemical signals released into the air. When some plants, such as tomatoes or tobacco, are attacked by insects, they release special chemicals called volatile organic compounds. Nearby plants can detect these chemicals and respond by producing their own defensive substances, preparing for an attack before it happens. Some of these chemicals also attract predators, such as wasps, that eat the insects attacking the plant. Communication also happens underground. The roots of many plants are connected to vast networks of fungi, sometimes called the 'wood wide web'. The fungi receive sugars from the plants, and in return, they help the plants absorb water and nutrients. Studies suggest that these networks can also carry warning signals and even move nutrients from one tree to another. In forests, for example, older 'mother trees' may support younger seedlings growing in the shade. However, some scientists warn that the popular idea of trees deliberately helping each other has been exaggerated, and that more research is needed. Finally, plants respond to sound and touch. Some plants grow stronger stems when they are regularly moved by wind. Understanding plant communication could help farmers protect crops with fewer chemical pesticides."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M19-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"Why does the teacher recommend learning words in phrases?","o":["It is faster to memorize lists.","Exams only test phrases.","It helps students use words correctly.","Phrases are shorter than words."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'This helps you use words correctly.'","h":"الاستخدام الصحيح."},{"n":89,"s":"What is spaced repetition?","o":["learning many words in one day","reviewing words at increasing intervals","reading the same page many times","writing words in different colours"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Reviewing after one day, three days, a week, a month.","h":"المراجعة على فترات متزايدة."},{"n":90,"s":"According to the teacher, why is testing yourself effective?","o":["Trying to recall a word is more effective than rereading it.","It is required by the exam.","It takes less time.","It replaces reading."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'Trying to recall a word is much more effective than simply rereading it.'","h":"الاسترجاع أكثر فاعلية."}],"t":"Talk: tips for learning new vocabulary","sc":["Situation: An English teacher speaks to students preparing for an exam. (225 words)","Lecturer: Good morning, everyone. Many of you have asked me how to learn vocabulary more effectively, so today I'll share some research-based tips. First, learn words in context, not in isolation. Instead of memorizing a list of single words, learn them in phrases or sentences. For example, don't just learn 'decision'; learn 'make a decision'. This helps you use words correctly. Second, use spaced repetition. If you review a new word once and never see it again, you'll probably forget it within a few days. Research shows that reviewing words at increasing intervals, for example after one day, three days, a week and then a month, helps move them into long-term memory. There are free apps that can organize this for you. Third, make the words personal. Write example sentences about your own life, your family or your interests. Personal connections make words more memorable. Fourth, read widely. Reading articles, stories and news in English exposes you to thousands of words in natural contexts, and you'll learn many of them without even trying. Finally, test yourself. Trying to recall a word is much more effective than simply rereading it. Cover the English word and try to remember it from the Arabic meaning, or from a picture. Learning vocabulary takes time, but if you study a little every day, you'll be surprised how quickly your vocabulary grows."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","On the other hand","As a result","For instance"],"x":["The company invested heavily in training its staff. ______, productivity increased by twenty per cent."],"k":2,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"Higher productivity is a result.","h":"النتيجة."},{"n":92,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) These cards can then be reviewed regularly until the words are remembered.","(2) Flashcards are a simple but effective way to learn vocabulary.","(3) On one side of each card, the learner writes a new word.","(4) On the other side, they write its meaning and an example sentence."],"k":0,"ty":"best order","e":"topic → one side → other side → review.","h":"ترتيب منطقي."},{"n":93,"s":"The (A) number of tourists (B) visiting AlUla (C) have grown (D) rapidly.","o":["number","visiting","have grown","rapidly"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"The number of … is singular: has grown.","h":"The number مفرد."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Saudi Arabia is developing its film industry. (2) New cinemas have opened in many cities since 2018. (3) Local filmmakers are receiving training and funding. (4) Popcorn is a popular snack in many countries. (5) Several Saudi films have won awards at international festivals."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Popcorn does not develop the point about the film industry.","h":"الفشار خارج الفكرة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Therefore","However","Similarly","For example"],"x":["Some people believe that money brings happiness. ______, research suggests that relationships and health matter more."],"k":1,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"A contrasting view.","h":"تضاد."},{"n":96,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Some books are very old.","Children enjoy reading clubs.","Internet access is common today.","Public libraries offer many valuable services to their communities."],"x":["______ They provide a quiet place to study, free internet access and thousands of books. Many also run workshops and reading clubs for children."],"k":3,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list library services.","h":"خدمات المكتبات."},{"n":97,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) After several hours, they finally reached the top.","(2) Early in the morning, the hikers began climbing Jabal Sawda.","(3) The path was steep and rocky, and they had to stop often to rest.","(4) From the summit, they could see clouds below them."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"start → difficulty → reached top → view.","h":"تسلسل زمني."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["For these reasons, electric cars are likely to become increasingly popular.","Petrol prices change every month.","Some cars are red.","Batteries are made in factories."],"x":["Electric cars produce no exhaust emissions and are cheaper to run than petrol cars. Battery technology is improving, and charging stations are becoming more common. Many governments also offer incentives to buyers. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A conclusion drawn from the reasons.","h":"خلاصة."},{"n":99,"s":"If he (A) had (B) studied harder, he (C) would have passed the exam (D) easy.","o":["had","studied","would have passed","easy"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"An adverb is needed: easily.","h":"الظرف: easily."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Phones are expensive to repair.","Some people sleep for ten hours.","Many apps are free.","The blue light from screens can affect the body's natural sleep cycle. Teacher Section — Mock 19"],"x":["Many people find it hard to sleep after using their phones at night. ______ Experts therefore recommend switching off screens at least an hour before bed."],"k":3,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"The cause leads to the recommendation.","h":"السبب قبل التوصية."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
